A||||7||Marie Elena Rivera||Throttle (I think?)
Z||000000||Marie Elena Rivera||04-16-99||08:28 AM||||My car does not want to stay idled.  I had no problem with it, until I had a problem with my coil pack & module (spelling??).  Now I get that fixed and I have a problem with the throttle.  When I start the car, it warms up and then just dies.  Since I got it fixed the other day this problem has started.  It even sticks, kind of.  When I am driving, especially in a higher gear #4, all of a sudden I notice that by hitting the gas the car does move anymore though it is still on, but by looking at the RPM's & Speedometert everything is kind of stuck, then, all of a sudden it will jolt forward and catch.  No indication lights come on, so I'll down shift, it will take a moment, but then finally catch & then die.  Then all the lights come on, and if I am lucky I can pop start it real quick.  But, it only works for a minute.  But last night, after I had it checked again, it was fine.  I stop at a friends house for about a half hour, come out, and the same thing.  Luckily I was able to "pop start" it home.  My mechanic says it is the throttle, and since its the fourth time this week I have brought it to him, he is going to go pick it up where it died this morning (and did not want to kick back over) and that he would fix the throttle.  So, if I didn't have this problem before, and it was my coil pack and module that got fixed, why do I have a different problem now?  And, is it the throttle?  I don't get it.  I have had many, many things fixed ont his car, and I am always right there with my mechanic.  It just seems that it is one right after the other.  It is a 1987 Pontiac Fiero (beautiful car), I love it.  I don't want to get rid of it.  Should I?  I have had a 1987 GrandAm engine in it, starter, clutch, idle motor, brakes, alternator, battery, fuel filter, etc. etc.  Please help.  I am going crazy.  Let me just state that I do not believe my mechanic is cheating me.  I have spent toooo many hours with him up there (I'm a woman and like to know what is being done to my car), so dress, heels and all, I'm in the garage under the hood as well), could it be that some mechanics just do not know about Fiero's?  Please help me.  Desperado en Harrisburg, Pennsylvania.  Marie Elena Rivera   
Z||000001||theogre||04-16-99||11:32 AM||||is it a 4 or 6 cyl?
Z||000002||Marie Elena Rivera||04-16-99||01:09 PM||||It is a 4 cylinder.  Marie Elena
Z||000003||theogre||04-16-99||02:13 PM||||well, on a 4cyl built prior to 87 I'd say check the "Tach Filter" which is known to short out and interfere with firing the coil, but so far I can't even seem to find it on my 87.<p>Does anyone know for sure if the DIS ingitions even have a tach filter and where it is, or is it embeded into the coil controller? I can't find an 87 specific schematic.<p>You might want to look at the no hot start thread. I think your problem could be related. We covered alot of ground in that thread.<p>The Fiero as a whole is quite differant, but the engine is common to many other GM cars. If your mech is any good with that engine in other cars, he should be able to fix it. The question is why were the coils, and controller, replaced? IF 87's still have the seperate tach filter you may have replaced the wrong parts.<p>((The Tach Filter is a device that "cleans up" the signal feed to the tachometer. It can fail in such a way that it will cause exactly these kinds of problems. Inside the filter are components that espcially don't like allot of heat. It can cause symtoms what look like bad coils and/or controller.))
Z||000004||Cliff Pennock||04-16-99||03:34 PM||||Speaking of tach-filters, I found the original schematic for it buried somewhere in the Haynes manual. Unfortunately, it did not list any component values...<br> <IMG SRC="http://www.pennock.nl/images/tachfilter.jpg"> 
Z||000005||theogre||04-16-99||04:25 PM||||the specific values really don't matter much. any of hundreds of combinations would work. <p>What matter are those 2 caps. if one of those is flaky, then you have a problem. because of the resisitors you don't get a dead short tho so the coil will still fire to a point. Since the engine needs more firing energy at higher RPM, or higher temp, it waits to go nuts untill the engine is warm.<p>Acording to the schematic for the whole system. the car should run without the filter, you just won't have a tach.
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||04-16-99||04:28 PM||||Ogre, could the throttle position sensor be causing these problems?
Z||000007||theogre||04-17-99||03:03 PM||||it could, but i think it would cause allot of stalling since the buterfly would open without a coresponding input of fuel the mix would go so lean it would likely die. A MAP unit or a Vacuum leak to it could cause this. The ECU would not know that engine load had changed if the MAP is flaky. A MAP that works at all often won't set any codes.<p>after this many years, i'd replace all those as a matter of course. rugedly made they are not. I've replaced pretty much all my main sensors. MAP, TPS, O2 are all new. So Is my injector. (replacing the injector finnaly killed my intermitant code 45's..... so much for the items in that error cause list.)<p>I'd also pull of the 700 series TBI, and put a kit in it, which includes a new presure regulator diaphram. I found out the hard way that the diaphram is most of the cost of the kit, so if you are replaceing it you might as well get the kit and clean the whole thing. ((87+ 4cyls use the 700 unit made only for GM. 86&down use the Bosh/Holley unit which is completely diferant.))
